      {
        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "274338dc-2696-5c3f-b4d0-37623ccd3169",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Karlheinz Pintsch, Rudolf Hess, Adolf Hitler\n- Places: Scotland, United Kingdom\n- Roles: Deputy Führer, Hitler's deputy\n- Topics: May 1941 flight, World War II, historiographical debate, crimes against peace, Mein Kampf\n\nFacts:\n- In 1933, Rudolf Hess was appointed Deputy Führer to Adolf Hitler.\n- Rudolf Hess edited Adolf Hitler's book Mein Kampf as Hitler's deputy.\n- In May 1941 during World War II, Rudolf Hess flew solo to Scotland in an attempt to negotiate peace with the United Kingdom.\n- Karlheinz Pintsch reported Rudolf Hess's illegal May 1941 flight to Scotland to Adolf Hitler.\n- Karlheinz Pintsch's recollections and notes about Rudolf Hess's May 1941 flight have been debated by historians.\n- After Rudolf Hess was taken prisoner, he was convicted of crimes against peace and served a life sentence until his suicide.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
        "original_id": "9d1828f6-3c1b-52e8-aab6-fb0f22b2149e",
        "text": "Page: Karlheinz Pintsch. Sentence: It fell to him to report Hess's illegal May 1941 flight to Scotland to Hitler and his recollections and notes have been the subject of debate by historians.\nPage: Rudolf Hess. Sentence: Appointed Deputy Führer to Adolf Hitler in 1933, he served in this position until 1941, when he flew solo to Scotland in an attempt to negotiate peace with the United Kingdom during World War II.\nPage: Rudolf Hess. Sentence: He was taken prisoner and eventually was convicted of crimes against peace, serving a life sentence until his suicide.\nPage: Mein Kampf. Sentence: The book was edited by Hitler's deputy Rudolf Hess.",
